LESSONS = [
    {
        'id': 'iam-authentication-access-models',
        'title': 'Authentication, Authorization and Access Control Models',
        'summary': 'How systems decide who you are and what you may do: factors, biometrics, Kerberos and network access protocols, and the DAC, MAC, RBAC, ABAC family.',
        'diagram': 'cisspAccessModels',
        'vocabIds': ['f1001', 'f1002', 'f1003', 'f1004', 'f1009', 'f1013', 'f1014', 'f1015', 'f1016'],
        'quizIds': ['q1001', 'q1002', 'q1004', 'q1005', 'q1006', 'q1007'],
        'reading': (
            "Every access decision starts with four ideas that are easy to blur together. Identification is claiming who you are, such as typing a username. Authentication is proving the claim. Authorization is the decision about what that proven identity may do. Accountability is being able to tie an action back to a person later through logs. The order matters on the exam: a system cannot authorize someone it has not authenticated, and accountability collapses if accounts are shared or the logs can be edited by the people being logged.\n\n"
            "Authentication relies on factors. Something you know is a password or PIN, something you have is a token, smart card or phone, and something you are is a biometric such as a fingerprint. Multi-factor authentication means at least two different categories, so a password plus a security question is still one factor used twice. Biometrics bring their own vocabulary. The false acceptance rate (FAR) counts impostors let in, the false rejection rate (FRR) counts legitimate users turned away, and the crossover error rate (CER) is the point where the two are equal. A lower CER means a more accurate device, and it is the fair way to compare products because FAR and FRR can be traded against each other by adjusting sensitivity.\n\n"
            "Kerberos is the network authentication protocol you must understand. A user proves identity once to the Authentication Service in the Key Distribution Center and receives a ticket-granting ticket. Whenever the user wants a service, that ticket is presented to the Ticket-Granting Service, which returns a service ticket for that one resource. The password never crosses the network after the first step, and the user does not keep typing it. For remote access, RADIUS provides authentication, authorization and accounting for network access with only the password encrypted, while TACACS+ separates the three functions and encrypts the entire session, which is why it suits administering routers and switches.\n\n"
            "Once identity is established, an access control model decides authorization. In discretionary access control (DAC) the owner of a resource decides who else may use it, which is flexible but lets permissions drift. In mandatory access control (MAC) the system compares a clearance on the subject with a label on the object and owners cannot change the outcome, which fits environments where confidentiality is everything. Role-based access control (RBAC) attaches permissions to job roles and gives users roles, making administration and least privilege manageable. Attribute-based access control (ABAC) evaluates policy over attributes of the user, the data, the action and the environment, so it can say yes only if the device is healthy, the data is tagged for a project, and the request is within business hours.\n\n"
            "Underneath all of the models are two principles. Least privilege gives people only what they need to do the job, and need-to-know limits access to the specific information required even for someone holding a clearance. Choose the model by asking where the decision should live. If it should live with the owner, think DAC. If it should be enforced by labels, think MAC. If it follows the job, think RBAC. If it depends on context that roles cannot capture, think ABAC."
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to access control? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': 'Picture entering a hospital. Telling the front desk your name is identification. Showing a photo ID and a badge that matches the list is authentication, and showing more than one kind of proof is multi-factor. What the badge opens afterward is authorization: a nurse can enter the ward but not the pharmacy vault. If the hospital lets each department head hand out keys, that is discretionary control. If the hospital stamps every document and badge with a security level and the doors enforce it, that is mandatory control. If doors open by job title, it is role-based, and if a door opens only for a doctor on duty, in the building, with a hospital-issued device, it is attribute-based.',
        'keyTerms': ['Identification', 'Authentication', 'Authorization', 'Accountability', 'Multi-factor authentication', 'Crossover error rate', 'Kerberos', 'TACACS+', 'DAC / MAC / RBAC / ABAC'],
        'commonTraps': [
            'A password and a security question are both knowledge factors, so the combination is not multi-factor authentication.',
            'FAR counts impostors accepted (the security failure) and FRR counts legitimate users rejected (the usability failure); the CER, where they are equal, is the number to compare devices.',
            'Rule-based and role-based sound alike but differ: rule-based applies global rules to everyone, role-based grants permissions by job function.',
            'In Kerberos the ticket-granting service issues the service ticket; the authentication service only issues the ticket-granting ticket.',
        ],
        'scenario': 'A regional bank wants administrators to manage its routers with per-command control and a full audit trail, while branch staff reach core banking applications only from compliant devices during working hours. The architect chooses TACACS+ for device administration, since it separates authorization and encrypts the entire session, and attribute-based policy for the applications, since device health and time of day cannot be expressed through static roles. Phishing-resistant security keys are issued for administrators.',
        'onTheJob': 'Most real programs use several models together: roles for the bulk of access, attributes for sensitive exceptions, and mandatory labels only where regulation demands them. When asked to choose, pay attention to what is changing. A requirement that mentions device health, location or time is pointing at ABAC or risk-based access. Be wary of biometric projects that skip the privacy question, since a leaked fingerprint template cannot be reset like a password. Finally, remember that strong authentication is wasted if the account lifecycle is sloppy, so connect this lesson to provisioning and reviews.',
    },
    {
        'id': 'iam-federation-lifecycle-pam',
        'title': 'Federation, Single Sign-On, Identity Lifecycle and Privileged Access',
        'summary': 'SAML, OAuth and OpenID Connect, what single sign-on really buys you, joiner-mover-leaver, access reviews and managing privileged accounts.',
        'diagram': 'cisspFederationFlow',
        'vocabIds': ['f1021', 'f1022', 'f1023', 'f1024', 'f1025', 'f1027', 'f1028', 'f1030'],
        'quizIds': ['q1009', 'q1010', 'q1011', 'q1012', 'q1013', 'q1014'],
        'reading': (
            "Federation solves a simple problem: people at one organization need to use applications run by another, and nobody wants a second account to forget and abandon. In a federation an identity provider (IdP) authenticates the user and vouches for them to a service provider (SP), which trusts the IdP instead of holding a password. The cost is concentration: the IdP becomes critical infrastructure, so it needs high availability, strong multi-factor authentication and close monitoring.\n\n"
            "Three standards dominate and are easy to mix up. SAML 2.0 is an XML-based standard that carries signed authentication and authorization assertions, mostly for browser single sign-on to enterprise and software-as-a-service applications. In a typical flow the user visits the SP, is redirected to the IdP, authenticates, and the IdP sends a signed assertion back through the browser. OAuth 2.0 is different in purpose: it is an authorization framework that lets an application obtain a scoped access token for another service, such as reading a calendar, without ever seeing the user's password. OpenID Connect sits on top of OAuth 2.0 and adds an ID token that says who authenticated, which is why modern sign-in with a corporate or social identity in web and mobile apps uses it. A useful memory hook is that OAuth authorizes, OpenID Connect authenticates, and SAML asserts identity for enterprise web single sign-on.\n\n"
            "Single sign-on (SSO) is the user-facing benefit of federation: authenticate once and move between applications. It cuts password fatigue and helpdesk calls and gives one place to enforce policy, including conditional access that steps up to extra verification when risk rises. The trade-off is that a single stolen credential or session can open everything behind it, so SSO should be paired with multi-factor authentication and sensible session limits.\n\n"
            "Identities have a lifecycle: joiner, mover, leaver. Accounts should be created from an authoritative source such as the human resources system, with approval from the data owner and only the access the role needs. Movers are the quiet danger. When someone changes jobs and keeps old access, privilege creep builds up until a person can both create and approve payments, a separation-of-duties failure. Leavers must have access removed promptly, and automation such as SCIM that is triggered by the HR event beats a manager remembering to send an email. Periodic access recertification asks managers and owners to confirm each entitlement, and reviews of privileged accounts should come more often than reviews of ordinary ones.\n\n"
            "Privileged accounts deserve extra control because their misuse is catastrophic. Privileged access management vaults credentials, checks them out for a limited time, records the session, rotates the secret afterward and removes standing administrator rights, replacing shared passwords with named accountability. Just-in-time elevation grants rights only for a task and then removes them. When an exam question describes shared admin passwords, stale accounts or users with accumulated rights, the answer is almost always a lifecycle or privileged-access control, not a stronger password policy."
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to federation? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': 'Think of a passport and a border officer. Your home government (the identity provider) checks who you are once and issues a passport. A foreign country (the service provider) never sees your birth certificate; it trusts the passport because it trusts the issuer and can check the seal, which is the digital signature. A visa that lets you work only in certain jobs and only for a few weeks is like an OAuth token: limited scope, limited time. Joiners get a passport, movers get a new visa and must hand back the old one, and leavers have the passport cancelled.',
        'keyTerms': ['Identity provider', 'Service provider', 'SAML', 'OAuth 2.0', 'OpenID Connect', 'Single sign-on', 'Joiner-mover-leaver', 'Privilege creep', 'Privileged access management'],
        'commonTraps': [
            'OAuth 2.0 is authorization, not authentication; add OpenID Connect when the application must know who the user is.',
            'Single sign-on reduces passwords but creates a concentrated target, so it demands strong MFA rather than relaxing it.',
            'Stronger passwords do not fix orphaned accounts or privilege creep; those are lifecycle and review problems.',
            'Outsourcing identity to a cloud provider does not outsource accountability for the policy and the data.',
        ],
        'scenario': 'After an acquisition, a manufacturer has 60 cloud applications, three directories and frequent password resets. An audit also finds former contractors with active accounts and a shared administrator login documented on a wiki. The security architect federates the applications to a single identity provider that enforces MFA, links account removal to the human resources termination record using automated provisioning, schedules quarterly recertification for privileged groups and puts administrator credentials into a vault with session recording.',
        'onTheJob': 'Start federation with the applications that cause the most helpdesk pain and the most risk, and plan for the identity provider outage before you move everything onto it, including break-glass accounts that are stored offline and monitored. The hardest part of lifecycle management is not technology but data: if the HR system is wrong about who is a contractor, automation will faithfully do the wrong thing. Make access reviews meaningful by showing reviewers who the person is and what the entitlement lets them do, since reviewers who see raw group names tend to approve everything.',
    },
    {
        'id': 'assessment-testing-audit',
        'title': 'Security Assessment, Testing and Audit',
        'summary': 'Designing assessment strategies, choosing between scanning, penetration testing and audits, software testing, metrics and turning results into decisions.',
        'diagram': 'cisspTestingLadder',
        'vocabIds': ['f1033', 'f1035', 'f1036', 'f1037', 'f1038', 'f1039', 'f1040', 'f1043', 'f1050'],
        'quizIds': ['q1017', 'q1018', 'q1019', 'q1021', 'q1024', 'q1025'],
        'reading': (
            "Assessment and testing answer one question: do our controls actually work? A good strategy matches the activity to the question being asked and the audience that needs the answer. An assessment is a broad evaluation of controls against requirements. A test exercises a specific control, such as a vulnerability scan or a phishing simulation. An audit is a formal, evidence-based examination against a defined standard, and its value rests on independence. Internal audits are inexpensive and useful for readiness, external audits carry credibility with regulators and customers, and third-party audits examine suppliers.\n\n"
            "When you rely on a service provider, you usually read their assurance reports rather than audit them yourself. SOC 1 covers controls relevant to financial reporting. SOC 2 covers the trust services criteria of security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality and privacy, and it is the report you normally want for a cloud or software-as-a-service vendor. SOC 3 is a short, public summary. Each of SOC 1 and SOC 2 comes in two types: Type I describes the design of controls at one date, while Type II also tests that they operated effectively over a period, so Type II gives much stronger assurance. Reading a report means checking the scope, the audit period, any exceptions and the controls the report expects customers to run themselves.\n\n"
            "Technical testing has a ladder of intensity. A vulnerability assessment uses scanners to find and rank weaknesses without necessarily exploiting them, and credentialed scans, which log in to inspect patches and settings, are more accurate than unauthenticated ones. A penetration test goes further by exploiting weaknesses to show real impact and how findings chain together, which is why it needs written authorization and rules of engagement covering scope, timing, permitted techniques and emergency contacts. Testers may be black box (no knowledge), gray box (partial) or white box (full knowledge). Red teams emulate adversaries to test detection and response, and purple teaming has attackers and defenders collaborate so each technique improves a detection. Social engineering and wireless tests cover people and radio signals that scanners cannot see.\n\n"
            "Software needs its own testing. Static analysis reads code, dynamic testing attacks the running application, and fuzzing feeds malformed input to find crashes. Misuse case and negative testing check that abuse fails, interface testing examines the trust boundaries where components meet, and regression testing makes sure fixes stay fixed. Coverage numbers tell you how much code ran, not whether the tests mattered.\n\n"
            "Finally, results must become decisions. Raw findings are validated to weed out false positives, rated with business context (a CVSS score measures technical severity in general, not your risk), and reported with an executive summary and clear owners. Key performance indicators show how well a process runs, while key risk indicators warn of rising exposure. Continuous monitoring replaces occasional snapshots with an ongoing stream of evidence. The consistent exam answer is that a report must reach the people who can act on it, in language they can use."
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to security testing? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': 'Imagine checking the safety of a building. A vulnerability scan is a clipboard walk-through that lists every unlocked window and cracked lock. A penetration test hires a locksmith, with written permission, to actually get in and show how far they can go. An audit is a certified inspector, independent of the building manager, comparing the building to the fire code. A SOC 2 Type II report is like an inspector who watched how the sprinklers and alarms were maintained for a year rather than checking them once on a single afternoon.',
        'keyTerms': ['Assessment vs. audit', 'SOC 2 Type II', 'Vulnerability scan', 'Penetration test', 'Rules of engagement', 'Black / gray / white box', 'CVSS', 'KPI and KRI', 'False positive'],
        'commonTraps': [
            'A Type II report beats a Type I report because it tests operating effectiveness over time, not just design on one day.',
            'A vulnerability scan finds weaknesses; only a penetration test demonstrates exploitation, and it needs written authorization first.',
            'CVSS is generic severity, not your risk; prioritize by asset value, exposure and active exploitation.',
            'An audit run by the people who operate the control lacks independence, however skilled they are.',
        ],
        'scenario': 'A payments company must show customers that its controls work, and also wants to know whether an attacker could reach cardholder data. The security manager commissions an independent SOC 2 Type II examination for customer assurance, schedules monthly credentialed scans for patch accuracy, and signs rules of engagement with an external team for an annual gray box penetration test. The test report arrives with an executive summary ranking findings by business impact and names an owner and due date for each fix.',
        'onTheJob': 'The most valuable habit is scoping. Many tests produce little value because nobody agreed what question they were answering. Before any penetration test, confirm the emergency stop contact and which production systems are fragile. Expect scanners to be noisy, so build a validation step into the process and track the false positive rate. When presenting to executives, show a trend and a decision, such as the percentage of critical systems patched within policy and what is needed to improve it, not a table of hosts. Keep evidence of control operation as you go, because audit season is miserable when you must reconstruct a year from memory.',
    },
    {
        'id': 'ops-monitoring-incident-forensics',
        'title': 'Logging, Monitoring, Incident Response and Forensics',
        'summary': 'Detecting attacks with logs, SIEM and endpoint tools, running the incident lifecycle, and preserving evidence so it can stand up in court.',
        'diagram': 'cisspIncidentResponse',
        'vocabIds': ['f1057', 'f1058', 'f1059', 'f1063', 'f1064', 'f1071', 'f1072', 'f1074', 'f1075'],
        'quizIds': ['q1031', 'q1033', 'q1034', 'q1035', 'q1037', 'q1039'],
        'reading': (
            "You cannot respond to what you cannot see, so security operations begins with logging and monitoring. Logs from servers, applications, firewalls and identity systems should flow quickly to a central, protected store, where a security information and event management (SIEM) platform normalizes and correlates them and raises alerts. Three details are tested often. Clocks must be synchronized to a trusted source so events from different systems can be lined up. Logs must be protected from alteration, ideally by sending them off the system immediately and keeping administrators away from the record of their own actions. Retention must follow policy and law. Around the SIEM sit other tools: intrusion detection systems alert and intrusion prevention systems sit in line and block, endpoint detection and response watches behavior on devices and can isolate them, user and entity behavior analytics flags deviations from normal, and orchestration tools automate repetitive response steps. A SIEM that is not tuned either buries analysts in noise or misses the real attack.\n\n"
            "An event is anything observable. An incident is an event or series of events that violates or threatens policy, so triage decides which events become incidents. The incident response lifecycle starts with preparation: the plan, the trained team with defined authority, tools, contacts and logging all have to exist before anything happens. Detection and analysis confirm what is going on and how serious it is. Containment limits spread, for example by isolating a host, while preserving evidence. Eradication removes the cause, such as malware and the access that let it in. Recovery restores systems, ideally from known-good sources, and monitors closely for reinfection. The final phase, post-incident review, asks what happened and what to change, without blame, and produces tracked improvements. The order matters: contain before eradicating, and eradicate before restoring.\n\n"
            "Some incidents become investigations, and then evidence handling decides whether findings can be used. The forensic process runs from identification and preservation through collection, examination, analysis and reporting. Evidence is collected in order of volatility, from the most fragile to the most durable: memory and running processes first, then network state, then disk, then logs and archives. Pulling the plug before capturing memory throws away the best evidence. Investigators never analyze the original. They create a bit-for-bit image through a hardware write blocker and prove the copy is identical by comparing cryptographic hashes. Chain of custody records every person who handled the evidence, when and why, and a gap can make it inadmissible.\n\n"
            "The type of case sets the rigor. Criminal matters need proof beyond reasonable doubt and the strictest handling, civil cases use the lower preponderance of evidence standard, and administrative or regulatory investigations follow internal policy or compliance rules. When litigation is reasonably expected, a legal hold suspends normal deletion so relevant records are preserved; destroying them afterwards is spoliation. Throughout, legal counsel and communications staff should be part of the team, and statements to outsiders should come from designated people."
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to incident response? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': 'Think of a hospital emergency department. Security cameras and monitors in every room are the logs, and the central nurses station watching them is the SIEM. Not every beep is an emergency: triage decides which events become incidents. Once there is one, the team stops the bleeding first (containment) before treating the underlying illness (eradication) and then helping the patient get back to normal life (recovery). Afterwards the doctors review the case to improve procedures. If a crime is involved, the clothes and samples are bagged, sealed and signed for at every handover, and the most perishable samples are taken first, which is chain of custody and order of volatility.',
        'keyTerms': ['SIEM', 'Event vs. incident', 'Incident response lifecycle', 'Containment', 'Eradication', 'Order of volatility', 'Chain of custody', 'Write blocker', 'Legal hold'],
        'commonTraps': [
            'Rebuilding or powering off a compromised host immediately destroys volatile evidence; contain and preserve first unless life safety demands otherwise.',
            'Lessons learned comes last and is not about blame, and preparation comes first and happens before any incident.',
            'Hash values prove the copy matches the original; chain of custody proves who handled it. Evidence needs both.',
            'An IDS only detects and alerts; an IPS sits in line and can block.',
        ],
        'scenario': 'A SIEM alert shows an engineering workstation beaconing to a known malicious address at night. The analyst confirms the activity, opens an incident and the responder isolates the machine from the network without powering it off. Memory is captured first, then a write-blocked disk image is made and hashed, with each transfer recorded on a custody form. After the cause (a phishing link) is removed and credentials are reset, the host is rebuilt from a clean image. A blameless review leads to better email filtering and a new detection rule.',
        'onTheJob': 'Practice the plan before you need it: tabletop exercises reveal missing phone numbers, unclear authority and the fact that nobody can remember who talks to the press. Keep an offline copy of the plan and contact list, since the systems you would read it on may be the ones affected. Tune detections continuously, retire rules that never fire usefully, and measure time to detect and time to contain. Involve legal counsel early, particularly where regulators or customers must be notified within deadlines, and write incident notes as if they will be read in court.',
    },
    {
        'id': 'ops-resilience-change-physical',
        'title': 'Resilience, Recovery, Change and Physical Operations',
        'summary': 'Recovery objectives, backups and alternate sites, testing the plan, configuration, patch and change management, and physical and personnel safety in daily operations.',
        'diagram': 'cisspBackupTypes',
        'vocabIds': ['f1076', 'f1077', 'f1078', 'f1080', 'f1081', 'f1083', 'f1084', 'f1085'],
        'quizIds': ['q1040', 'q1041', 'q1042', 'q1043', 'q1044', 'q1045'],
        'reading': (
            "Resilience planning starts with the business, not the technology. A business impact analysis identifies the critical processes and sets the numbers that drive everything else. The maximum tolerable downtime (MTD, also called maximum allowable outage) is how long a process can be down before the organization is in serious trouble. The recovery time objective (RTO) is the target to restore the service, and it plus the work recovery time needed to verify data and resume work must fit inside the MTD. The recovery point objective (RPO) is how much data you can afford to lose, measured in time, and it sets how often you must back up or replicate. A business continuity plan keeps critical business functions running through any disruption. A disaster recovery plan is the technology-focused part that restores systems and data.\n\n"
            "Backups implement the RPO. A full backup copies everything. An incremental backup copies what changed since the last backup of any type, so it is quick to take but a restore needs the last full plus every incremental since. A differential copies what changed since the last full, so it grows each day but a restore needs only the last full and the latest differential. Follow the 3-2-1 idea of three copies, two media types and one offsite, and make at least one copy immutable or offline, because ransomware hunts for reachable backups. RAID and clustering are redundancy for availability, not backup, since they faithfully copy deletions and encryption. Above all, test restores; a backup that has never been restored is only a hope.\n\n"
            "The RTO selects the alternate site. A hot site is fully equipped with current data and can take over in minutes to hours. A warm site has hardware and connectivity but needs data and configuration brought in. A cold site offers only space, power and cooling, and is cheap but slow. Plans are proven through a ladder of tests: a read-through of the document, a tabletop or walkthrough discussion, a simulation, a parallel test that recovers at the alternate site while production keeps running, and a full interruption test that actually switches production, which carries the most risk.\n\n"
            "Day-to-day operations keep that environment predictable. Configuration management defines secure baselines, tracks every item in an inventory and detects drift. Patch management identifies, tests, deploys and verifies updates, with a fast path for actively exploited flaws. Change management routes alterations through request, risk review, approval, implementation, verification and documentation, often with a change advisory board, and defines an emergency path with review after the fact. Security belongs in this process because unreviewed changes are how outages and exposures arrive.\n\n"
            "Physical and personnel matters complete the picture. Defense in depth places layers from the perimeter to the room to the rack. Pre-action sprinklers are the common choice for data centers because water needs two triggers, and gas agents protect without water damage but must be safe for people. Life safety comes first: evacuation, duress codes, travel briefings and workplace violence prevention protect people before property."
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to disaster recovery? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': 'Consider a restaurant. The business impact analysis says the kitchen can be closed for about four hours on a Saturday night before regulars are lost, which is the maximum tolerable downtime. The recovery time objective is how fast the owner promises to reopen, and the recovery point objective is how many orders written down since the last time they were safely copied can be lost. A hot site is a second kitchen, fully stocked and running next door. A warm site is an empty kitchen with ovens installed. A cold site is a rented room with a power outlet. Change management is the rule that nobody rearranges the kitchen during service without telling the head chef.',
        'keyTerms': ['Business impact analysis', 'MTD / RTO / RPO / WRT', 'Incremental vs. differential', '3-2-1 rule', 'Hot / warm / cold site', 'Parallel test', 'Configuration baseline', 'Change advisory board', 'Pre-action sprinkler'],
        'commonTraps': [
            'The RTO plus work recovery time must fit within the MTD; the MTD is the outer limit, not the target.',
            'Incremental is quickest to back up but slowest to restore; differential is larger to back up but faster to restore.',
            'RAID and clustering protect availability from hardware failure; they do not protect against deletion, corruption or ransomware.',
            'A parallel test does not interrupt production; a full interruption test does and is the riskiest.',
        ],
        'scenario': 'A hospital billing system has an MTD of eight hours, an RTO of four and an RPO of 30 minutes. The team replicates the database to a warm site every ten minutes, keeps nightly backups with an offline copy, and rehearses a parallel failover twice a year. A critical remote-code-execution patch is released on a Tuesday, so the team uses the emergency change path: a rapid test on a staging replica, deployment with a rollback plan, and a retrospective review at the next advisory board meeting.',
        'onTheJob': 'Most recovery plans fail on dependencies nobody wrote down: the directory service, the licensing server, the person who knows a password. Map them during the business impact analysis, and rehearse with the staff who will actually be on call, not the people who wrote the plan. Treat backup restoration time as a measured number, because data volumes grow and yesterday\'s RTO silently becomes unachievable. Keep your change calendar visible to security so that detections of unexpected change have context, and make the emergency path real but costly enough that it is not used for convenience.',
    },
    {
        'id': 'sdlc-secure-development',
        'title': 'Secure Software Development and Acquired Software',
        'summary': 'Building security into development life cycles and DevSecOps, common coding flaws and defenses, application security testing, and trusting software you did not write.',
        'diagram': 'cisspSecureSdlc',
        'vocabIds': ['f1092', 'f1094', 'f1099', 'f1101', 'f1102', 'f1107', 'f1109', 'f1110', 'f1113'],
        'quizIds': ['q1047', 'q1048', 'q1049', 'q1052', 'q1053', 'q1054'],
        'reading': (
            "A software development life cycle is the sequence of requirements, design, development, testing, deployment and maintenance through which software passes, and the central idea of secure development is that security belongs in every phase rather than at the end. Security requirements are written with the functional ones, threat modeling examines the design before code exists, developers follow secure coding standards, testing includes security tests, deployment is hardened, and maintenance includes patching and eventually secure retirement. The reason is cost: a design flaw found in review is cheap to fix, and the same flaw found after release can be ruinous. Moving security earlier is called shifting left.\n\n"
            "Methodologies change how this is done but not whether. Waterfall completes phases in order with heavy documentation. Agile works in short iterations, so security has to appear as stories, acceptance criteria and automated tests inside each sprint. DevOps joins development and operations through automation, and DevSecOps adds security checks, policy and testing into the same pipeline so feedback is fast. The pipeline itself becomes a target, because whoever controls it controls what ships; protect its credentials, require review and approval, and sign and verify build artifacts.\n\n"
            "Threat modeling with STRIDE asks, for each component and data flow, about spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service and elevation of privilege. Coding flaws are the other half. SQL injection, in which input alters a database query, is stopped by parameterized queries and least-privileged database accounts. Cross-site scripting, in which a victim's browser runs attacker script, is stopped by output encoding appropriate to the context, supported by a content security policy. Cross-site request forgery, in which a logged-in browser is tricked into sending a request, is stopped by anti-forgery tokens and SameSite cookies. Buffer overflows, caused by writing past the end of a buffer, are reduced by bounds checking and memory-safe languages. A recurring principle is to validate input on the server using allow lists and to encode output for where it lands.\n\n"
            "Testing complements design. Static analysis reads source code early and points to the line at fault. Dynamic testing attacks the running application and finds configuration and runtime problems. Interactive testing uses instrumentation inside the app during tests. Software composition analysis inventories open-source components and flags known vulnerable versions, and a software bill of materials is the formal list that lets you answer quickly whether a newly announced flaw affects you.\n\n"
            "Most software today is partly or wholly written by someone else, and responsibility does not transfer with the purchase. Assess commercial, open-source and software-as-a-service products for the vendor's development practices, testing evidence, patch commitments, licensing and data handling before you adopt them. Protect the supply chain by verifying signatures and provenance, pinning and monitoring dependencies, and controlling who can publish builds. Code signing proves who published code and that it was unchanged, but not that it is safe."
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to secure development? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': 'Think of building a house. An architect who thinks about burglars while drawing the plans can put windows in sensible places for almost nothing. A building inspector who notices the problem after the walls are up has to tear things out. That is shifting left. Using pre-made components such as windows or wiring means trusting the manufacturer, so you check certificates and keep a list of what went in, which is the bill of materials, so that a recall notice can be matched to your house in minutes.',
        'keyTerms': ['SDLC', 'Shift left', 'DevSecOps', 'STRIDE', 'SQL injection', 'Cross-site scripting', 'SAST / DAST / IAST', 'Software bill of materials', 'Supply chain security'],
        'commonTraps': [
            'Security reviews only at the end of a cycle find flaws when they are most expensive to fix; the right answer shifts security earlier.',
            'Parameterized queries beat escaping or blocklisting for SQL injection, and contextual output encoding is the primary defense for cross-site scripting.',
            'A web application firewall is a compensating control, not a substitute for fixing the vulnerable code.',
            'Code signing proves origin and integrity, not that the code is free of malware or flaws.',
        ],
        'scenario': 'A software company ships every two weeks. Its security team adds a threat-modeling checklist to design reviews and writes security acceptance criteria into stories. The pipeline runs static analysis and secret scanning on every commit, composition analysis on dependencies, and dynamic scans against a test environment before release, then signs the artifact. When a flaw in a popular open-source library is announced, the generated software bill of materials shows within minutes which three products are affected.',
        'onTheJob': 'Tools only help if developers trust them. Start with a small set of high-confidence rules so that findings are real, route results into the tracker developers already use, and measure the time to fix instead of the count of findings. Secure coding guidance works best as short, concrete patterns in the languages your teams use. For acquired software, put security requirements in the contract and request the vendor\'s testing evidence before you sign; after signing you have much less leverage. And treat the build system as production, with its own access control, logging and monitoring.',
    },
]

